course: biology
lesson: 317
title: Rodents of every kind: squirrels, marmots, prairie dogs, porcupines, chinchillas and dormice
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rodents have spread into almost every way of life: some glide between trees at night, some live in huge towns underground, some wear armour of quills, and some sleep away half the year.
explain: The squirrel family alone holds tree squirrels, flying squirrels, chipmunks, marmots and prairie dogs, which are really ground squirrels that bark.
explain: Porcupines carry thousands of barbed quills, chinchillas wear one of the thickest furs on Earth, and the tiny hazel dormouse curls into a tight golden ball to sleep through winter.
explain: Watch a grey squirrel in a park in autumn: it buries a nut, looks round, and sometimes digs a fake hole somewhere else. Every nut it forgets may grow into a tree.
fact: Grey squirrels bury thousands of nuts in autumn, each in a different place, and find many by memory and smell; the nuts they forget can grow into trees.
fact: Squirrels have been reported to pretend to bury a nut when another animal is watching.
fact: Flying squirrels glide from tree to tree on a flap of skin between their front and back legs, so they need not climb down where predators wait; in 2019 scientists found their fur glows pink under ultraviolet light, and nobody knows why.
fact: Alpine marmots are big ground squirrels that whistle to warn each other of eagles and foxes and hibernate for about six months in family groups; the American groundhog is a marmot too.
fact: On Groundhog Day in America people pretend a groundhog predicts when spring will come, but no animal can really forecast the weather weeks ahead.
fact: Prairie dogs are ground squirrels living in huge underground towns with many entrances and rooms; in about 1900 one town in Texas was reported to hold hundreds of millions of them.
fact: The scientist Con Slobodchikoff reported that prairie dog alarm calls change for hawks, coyotes, dogs and people, and even with the colour of a person's shirt; how much the calls really describe is still argued.
fact: A North American porcupine has about 30,000 quills, stiff hairs with tiny barbs that work their way into skin; porcupines cannot shoot their quills.
fact: African crested porcupines rattle hollow tail quills as a warning before they ever charge; they live on the ground, while American porcupines climb trees and some have gripping tails.
fact: The American and the African and Asian porcupines are only distant relatives, and many scientists think they grew quills separately.
fact: Chinchillas of the high Andes have one of the thickest furs of any land animal, with dozens of hairs from each tiny hole in the skin, and they bathe in dust because wet fur dries too slowly.
fact: Millions of chinchillas were hunted for fur coats in the 1800s and 1900s; both wild kinds are now endangered, and most pet chinchillas come from a few taken to the USA in the 1920s.
fact: The hazel dormouse gets very fat in autumn on hazelnuts and berries and sleeps through winter for up to about six months; hazel dormice in Britain have fallen by about half since 2000, reported.
fact: A dormouse leaves a smooth round hole with tooth marks around the edge in a hazelnut; volunteers hunt for nibbled nuts to find where rare dormice live.
exercise: How do grey squirrels find the nuts they buried? | By memory and smell | By marking each spot with a stone | They never find any again | By asking other squirrels
exercise: What can happen to a nut a squirrel forgets? | It can grow into a tree | It slowly turns into a stone | It is always eaten by worms | It floats away in the rain
exercise: Why might a squirrel pretend to bury a nut? | To fool another animal that is watching | To practise digging holes for the cold winter | Because it forgot the nut | To warm up its paws
exercise: How does a flying squirrel get from tree to tree? | It glides on a flap of skin between its legs | It flaps a pair of small feathered wings | It jumps with very long back legs only | It swings on its tail like a monkey
exercise: What did scientists find in 2019 about flying squirrel fur? | It glows pink under ultraviolet light | It changes colour every day | It is made of feathers | It keeps them dry under water
exercise: Why do Alpine marmots whistle? | To warn each other of eagles and foxes | To call the spring | To sing soft songs to their babies at night | To scare away the snow
exercise: How long do Alpine marmots hibernate? | About six months | Only about six days | About six whole years | They never hibernate
exercise: What kind of animal is a prairie dog? | A ground squirrel | A small wild dog of the plains | A kind of wild rabbit | A kind of blind mole
exercise: Where do prairie dogs live? | In huge underground towns with many entrances | In big round nests right at the tops of tall trees | In rivers like beavers | In caves with bats
exercise: What did Con Slobodchikoff report about prairie dog alarm calls? | They change for different dangers, even a shirt's colour | They are always exactly the same, whatever the danger is | They are only made at night | They copy the songs of birds
exercise: About how many quills does a North American porcupine have? | About 30,000 | About 300 in all | Only about 30 | About 3 million
exercise: Can a porcupine shoot its quills at an enemy? | No, the barbed quills stick in when touched | Yes, it can fire them up to about 10 metres | Yes, but only when asleep | Yes, out of its nose
exercise: What does an African crested porcupine do before it charges? | Rattles its hollow tail quills as a warning | Rolls up into a ball | Plays dead | Climbs the nearest tree
exercise: Which porcupines climb trees? | American porcupines | African crested porcupines | Asian brush-tailed porcupines | None, all live underground
exercise: Why do chinchillas bathe in dust instead of water? | Their thick fur dries too slowly when wet | Water is poisonous to them, even a single drop | They are scared of their reflection | Dust makes their fur grow
exercise: Why are wild chinchillas endangered? | Millions were hunted for fur coats | They eat only one rare flower | Cats were brought to the Andes | They forgot how to dig
exercise: Why does a hazel dormouse get very fat in autumn? | It needs the fat to last through about six months of winter sleep | It is getting ready to fly south over the sea to Africa for the winter | It eats less in autumn | Fat keeps it from being seen
exercise: How can a nibbled hazelnut show a dormouse lives nearby? | It has a smooth round hole with tooth marks round the edge | It is cracked neatly in half, the way a squirrel cracks nuts | It is wrapped in leaves | It is buried with a stone on top
test: In Ice Age, Scrat the squirrel spends the whole film chasing a single acorn. What do real grey squirrels do? | Bury thousands of nuts in different places and find many by memory and smell | Keep just one nut for their whole life and guard it from every other squirrel | Never store any food for winter | Bury all their nuts in one big hole
test: Count them: 4 marmots stand up on their back legs to look around. How many front paws are off the ground? | 8 | 4 | 16 | 12
test: Count them: a flying squirrel has a skin flap on each side between its front and back legs. How many flaps do 3 flying squirrels have? | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: «groundhog saw his shadow so 6 more weeks winter, proper science» What is true? | It is a fun custom; no animal can forecast the weather weeks ahead | True, groundhogs feel the coming weather in their fur and are never wrong | True, but only in America | It is science, but only when it is sunny
test: «mate reckons porcupines fire their spikes like arrows» What is right? | They cannot; the barbed quills stick in when touched | True, they can shoot them the length of a football pitch | True, but only the baby ones | True, they reload every hour
test: What would happen to a chinchilla given a bath in water every day? | Its thick fur would stay damp, which is bad for it | It would grow longer and thicker fur to keep the water out | Nothing, chinchillas love swimming | It would turn into a guinea pig
test: «in alvin and the chipmunks they sing — real chipmunks talk?» What is the truth? | Chipmunks are squirrels that make chirps and calls, not words | Chipmunks sing pop songs in the wild, just like in the cartoon | Chipmunks are birds that copy songs | Chipmunks make no sound at all
test: «why would a squirrel fly down the tree when it can just climb» What is the best answer? | Gliding keeps it away from the ground, where predators wait | Climbing wears down its front teeth, so it saves them by flying | Flying squirrels have no claws at all | It is too lazy to climb
test: A prairie dog town has many eyes and many holes. Why is that safer? | Someone spots danger quickly and everyone has a hole close by | Predators get muddled and cannot count how many holes there are | The holes are full of water | Big towns have no predators near them
test: In a story the Dormouse at the Mad Hatter's tea party keeps falling asleep. What is true of real hazel dormice? | They sleep through winter for up to about six months | They never sleep at all, not even in the coldest winter | They sleep only during tea | They sleep standing on their tails
test: Why might two groups of porcupines far apart both have quills, if they are only distant relatives? | Quills helped both stay safe, so both grew them separately, many scientists think | One group swam across the wide sea every year to visit the other and share quills | Quills are glued on by people | They swapped quills long ago
exam: Identify the nature of the animal commonly designated the prairie dog. | A ground squirrel which dwells in extensive subterranean colonies | A canine which dwells in burrows upon the prairie | A marsupial which carries its young in a pouch | A species of bird which nests in the ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the quills of the North American porcupine. | They number approximately thirty thousand and are barbed hairs which cannot be projected | They number approximately thirty and may be discharged at an assailant from a considerable distance | They are composed of bone and are shed annually | They are venomous spines identical to those of fish
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present status of wild chinchillas? | Both wild species are endangered following extensive hunting for fur | They are abundant and their numbers are increasing | They became extinct in the wild before 1800 | They have never been hunted by human beings
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the hazel dormouse accumulates considerable fat during the autumn. | It sustains the animal through hibernation lasting up to approximately six months | It permits the animal to float upon water during its long autumn migration across the sea | It serves as a store of heat for its young in summer | It is an indication of disease
